<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet xml:id="biostats-act-11" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>🏫 Activity 11 - Hypothesis Testing on Two Parameters </title>

    <introduction>
        <p> This assignment is due at the beginning of class on <alert> Wednesday, October 21 </alert>. It is graded based on completeness according to the rubric available on Canvas. You are encouraged to use your notes and collaborate with your group members to complete this activity. </p>

        <p> <alert> This activity is part of your final project. </alert> Extra care should be taken in completing this activity. </p>
    </introduction>

   <exercise>
        <introduction>
        <p>
            <alert>Prep Work:</alert>  Record your answers to the following prompts in your prep work journal. Your answers should be the <alert> only </alert> material entered into your journal. Do <alert> not </alert> include supporting definitions, theorems, examples, or remarks of any kind. 
        </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Write the formula for computing a pooled proportion.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="0.75in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Write the formula for the standard error in the sampling distribution for one proporiton and a difference of proportions below. What are the similarities and differences you notice.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="0.75in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Write the formula for the standard error in the sampling distribution for one mean and a difference of means below. What are the similarities and differences you notice.  
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                <alert> This problem is a portion of your final project </alert> and will involve heavier use of RStudio. Please save all of the code for this problem in an R script named <c>02_LASTNAME_FIRSTNAME_Activity11.r</c>. When you are finished with the problem, save your changes and submit the R script to the corresponding Activity 10 assignment in Canvas. If you are the group leader, you will also submit a copy of the written report for this section to the assignment on Canvas called "Hypothesis Testing on Two Parameters Check-in". 
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    As a group, choose a target qualitative variable from your data set. Then, identify another quantiative variable that you suspect may influence the value of the target variable.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Develop a sampling method to separate your data into two groups based on the <q>influencing</q> variable from part (a). Then use the command <c>dplyr::filter()</c> to split up your data set into your experimental groups in R. 
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Extract and store the target qualitative variable (as chosen in part (a)) from each experiment group. Remove any missing data with the <c>na.omit()</c> command. 
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use the <c>table()</c> command to build a frequency distribution for each of your samples. 
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Summarize this information in a written report. Your report should speak to each of the following prompts. 
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Clearly state your group's null and alternative hypotheses in both words and symbols. 
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Describe your sampling technique and determine whether it qualifies as a true random, systematic, stratified, or clustered sample. Explain how you implemented this in R. 
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Include the frequency distributions you generated. 
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Test your hypothesis with 95% confidence. Clearly distinguish between your sample statistics, point estimate, test statistic, critical value, standard error, margin of error, and confidence interval.  Interpret your results in plain language.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </task>
    </exercise>

</worksheet>
